WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.220
When you sent me this paper last week, you attached this little note to it.

00:04.340 --> 00:09.020
Oh, yeah. I remember. I was pretty frustrated that day.

00:09.120 --> 00:14.880
You really were. You said you were trying to optimize the server configurations for your team's new project,

00:15.120 --> 00:18.460
and you were just, you know, completely pulling your hair out over the cooling costs.

00:18.580 --> 00:19.900
Oh, it's a nightmare. Truly.

00:20.100 --> 00:21.560
Yeah, you said, and I'm quoting here,

00:21.760 --> 00:25.140
we're spending more energy chilling the room than doing the actual math.

00:25.220 --> 00:27.560
Yep. That is exactly what it feels like.

00:27.560 --> 00:32.040
Well, I've got some news for you, because according to the research we are unpacking today,

00:32.440 --> 00:36.160
your problem isn't that your cooling system is inefficient.

00:36.480 --> 00:36.880
No.

00:37.140 --> 00:42.640
No. Your problem is that you are looking at the fundamental physics of computation entirely backward.

00:43.040 --> 00:44.540
Okay. That's a bold claim.

00:44.740 --> 00:50.480
It is. But to understand why, I want you and really everyone listening to this deep dive

00:50.480 --> 00:53.060
to step outside of that server room for a second.

00:53.540 --> 00:57.440
Picture a massive, sprawling, hyperscale data center.

00:57.440 --> 00:59.380
Like the ones out in the desert or up north?

00:59.520 --> 01:03.640
Right. Let's go up north. It's the middle of January, somewhere in the northern hemisphere.

01:04.200 --> 01:07.560
Let's say a place where the winter air just bite through your coat.

01:07.740 --> 01:08.880
Oh, I know that kind of cold.

01:09.020 --> 01:13.080
Yeah. So inside this data center, millions of dollars are being burned,

01:13.380 --> 01:18.600
not just on the racks of processors, but on these giant industrial cooling systems.

01:18.600 --> 01:21.860
The HVAC on those buildings is unbelievable.

01:22.320 --> 01:28.760
Exactly. Massive fans, intricate chilled water loops, refrigerants, all working overtime.

01:29.140 --> 01:30.560
Definitely loud, right?

01:30.760 --> 01:37.520
And they are doing all of this just to capture the immense amount of thermal energy being generated by those servers.

01:37.640 --> 01:40.880
And then they violently vent it out into that freezing winter air.

01:41.020 --> 01:44.660
Yes. They are literally paying a fortune to throw heat away.

01:44.660 --> 01:45.940
Now look across the street.

01:46.080 --> 01:46.180
Right.

01:46.600 --> 01:48.220
There is a residential apartment building.

01:48.760 --> 01:54.020
Inside, the property managers and the tenants are also spending an absolute fortune.

01:54.220 --> 01:55.460
Because they're trying to stay warm.

01:55.620 --> 01:57.620
Right. They are burning natural gas.

01:57.720 --> 02:00.220
They are spinning electrical meters, running boilers,

02:00.280 --> 02:04.460
all to generate heat just to survive that exact same freezing winter outside.

02:04.460 --> 02:12.520
So you have one massive facility spending millions to get rid of heat and another building 50 yards away spending millions to create it.

02:12.720 --> 02:13.180
Exactly.

02:13.460 --> 02:18.140
It is honestly the absolute definition of a civilization scale blind spot.

02:18.800 --> 02:24.480
I mean, we've built an entire global infrastructure around ignoring this juxtaposition.

02:24.600 --> 02:25.680
We really have.

02:25.680 --> 02:32.580
We treat these two physical realities, the creation of computational heat and the desperation for physical warmth,

02:33.040 --> 02:37.240
as if they exist in, well, completely separate, non-overlapping universes.

02:37.400 --> 02:39.780
Which is exactly why we're doing this deep dive today.

02:39.940 --> 02:45.800
So the paper you sent over, it's an independent research piece published in June 2026 by an author named Fliction.

02:46.480 --> 02:48.460
The title is a bit of a mouthful, I have to admit.

02:48.460 --> 02:51.720
It is. It's called the xylomorphic premise.

02:52.340 --> 02:56.740
Waste heat, physical co-computation, and the architecture of post-carbon infrastructure.

02:56.980 --> 03:02.080
Right. And I know the language is dense, but the underlying argument is incredibly lucid.

03:02.200 --> 03:03.820
Yeah, it really cuts to the core of the issue.

03:04.180 --> 03:04.740
It does.

03:05.640 --> 03:09.520
Fliction is challenging the bedrock assumptions of modern engineering here.

03:09.880 --> 03:10.080
Oh.

03:10.500 --> 03:17.480
I mean, this paper isn't suggesting a, you know, a slight optimization to a server rack or some new type of thermal paste.

03:17.480 --> 03:18.540
No, not at all.

03:18.660 --> 03:26.080
It is proposing a radical, ground-up redesign of what a computer actually is, and by extension, what our cities could be.

03:26.540 --> 03:30.120
So our mission today is to explore this blueprint.

03:30.860 --> 03:35.660
We are going to look at how treating our computer networks like a biological forest,

03:35.680 --> 03:41.900
and we will definitely get into the actual mechanics of what that means, could solve two massive crises simultaneously.

03:41.900 --> 03:49.260
Right. The skyrocketing energy demand of global AI computing and the immense, stubborn energy cost of residential heating.

03:49.460 --> 03:56.380
Yeah. And as we navigate this, I want you to keep in mind that Fliction isn't merely suggesting we pipe some hot air from point A to point B.

03:56.460 --> 03:57.600
No, it's much deeper than that.

03:57.660 --> 03:57.780
Yeah.

03:58.000 --> 04:04.960
The author is arguing that our current assumption that functional specialization is always the most efficient way to build things is just a historical accident.

04:04.960 --> 04:09.700
Right. We've chosen to segregate these functions, which means we have the agency to integrate them.

04:10.020 --> 04:14.520
Let's start with that segregation, actually, because to understand why we need a radical redesign,

04:14.840 --> 04:18.940
we have to look at the invisible flaw in how we currently build our world.

04:19.060 --> 04:22.500
Right. What Fliction calls the separated substrate problem.

04:22.660 --> 04:30.020
Yes. So what exactly is a separated substrate and why is it failing us so badly right now?

04:30.020 --> 04:38.940
Well, the separated substrate problem is basically born from the organizing principle of almost all human infrastructure, which is functional separation.

04:39.480 --> 04:40.580
We do love our categories.

04:40.800 --> 04:47.040
We really do. We love categorization. Like we build a power plan and we optimize it purely to produce electricity.

04:47.260 --> 04:47.360
Right.

04:47.500 --> 04:54.780
We build a refrigerator and we optimize it purely to produce cold, entirely ignoring the heat it blows out the back into the kitchen.

04:54.900 --> 04:57.940
Oh, that is such a good point. You never think about the back of the fridge.

04:57.940 --> 05:03.620
Right. We evaluate these massive systems based on a single isolated metric.

05:03.720 --> 05:09.380
It's like a single output illusion. We grade the machine on one subject and just ignore the rest of the report card.

05:09.720 --> 05:12.540
That's a perfect way to put it. Take your data center example from earlier.

05:13.340 --> 05:19.820
In our current paradigm, a data center's success is evaluated solely by computational throughput per watt.

05:20.160 --> 05:24.640
Like how many floating point operations can you squeeze out of a megawatt of electricity?

05:24.640 --> 05:32.780
Exactly. The thermal energy produced by those processors is viewed entirely as an externality. It's an annoyance.

05:32.940 --> 05:34.480
A bug, not a feature.

05:34.660 --> 05:38.620
Right. A constraint that just has to be managed, chilled, and dumped.

05:38.800 --> 05:40.680
We literally call it waste heat.

05:41.160 --> 05:44.840
I mean, the terminology itself conditions us to think of it as garbage.

05:44.840 --> 05:54.660
It absolutely does. And then, flip the lens to the residential heating system in a house, it is evaluated solely by thermal output per unit of fuel consumed.

05:55.060 --> 05:57.600
How fast does the living room reach 70 degrees?

05:57.840 --> 06:07.200
Exactly. The fact that your basement furnace produces absolutely zero computation is not considered a failure because, well, nobody ever asked it to compute anything.

06:07.200 --> 06:09.680
Right. No one expects their boiler to process a spreadsheet.

06:10.060 --> 06:13.520
No. So we have these two completely isolated domains.

06:14.260 --> 06:20.540
And the paper uses some formal math to map this out, which I actually found super helpful for visualizing the disconnect.

06:20.880 --> 06:26.160
Yeah. The author introduces two fields, the waste heat field and the heat demand field.

06:26.420 --> 06:29.900
Right. Let's break down the notation real quick because it clarifies the physical layout.

06:29.900 --> 06:39.380
Yeah. The waste heat field, denoted as Q sub C, represents all the thermal energy generated by computation at a specific geographic location and time.

06:39.580 --> 06:41.120
Okay. Q sub C. Got it.

06:41.460 --> 06:49.780
Then you have the heat demand field Q sub H, which is the amount of space heating required at a specific location and time to keep humans alive and comfortable.

06:49.960 --> 06:52.900
And the kicker here is the relationship between those two variables.

06:52.900 --> 06:57.900
In our current infrastructure, Fliction points out they are statistically independent by design.

06:58.180 --> 07:03.280
Right. The formula is Q sub C is orthogonal to Q sub H. They don't interact at all.

07:03.360 --> 07:05.200
They just exist in parallel universes.

07:05.380 --> 07:09.940
Pretty much. Computation happens wherever the economic conditions for data centers are favorable.

07:10.140 --> 07:17.180
Which might mean like a region with massive tax breaks or access to a glacial river for cheap water cooling.

07:17.520 --> 07:20.920
Exactly. Or simply a massive plot of inexpensive land.

07:20.920 --> 07:24.620
Heat demand, however, happens wherever humans congregate.

07:24.880 --> 07:26.100
Right. In cities and towns.

07:26.260 --> 07:31.500
Right. And if those two fields happen to overlap, it's pure geographical coincidence.

07:32.060 --> 07:37.900
There is zero intentional engineering connecting the Q sub C field to the Q sub H field.

07:37.980 --> 07:42.120
Okay. Let me push back on that a bit because I'm putting myself in the shoes of someone listening right now.

07:42.140 --> 07:42.800
Sure. Go for it.

07:42.800 --> 07:51.920
Are we really just ignoring this massive energy resource because utility companies and tech giants exist in different zoning districts?

07:52.140 --> 08:03.360
I mean, I've read plenty of case studies specifically out of Scandinavia, actually, where they are taking the exhaust water from data centers and routing it into the local town's district heating loop.

08:03.360 --> 08:04.840
To warm up people's apartments, right?

08:04.840 --> 08:11.320
Yeah. Isn't that already happening? Doesn't plugging a pipe from the data center into the building solve the separated substrate problem?

08:11.440 --> 08:16.160
It's a really common counterargument. And fliction explicitly deconstructs it in the paper.

08:16.380 --> 08:16.800
Oh, really?

08:17.160 --> 08:23.860
Yeah. To explain why that is a band-aid rather than a cure, the paper breaks down infrastructural evolution into stages.

08:24.060 --> 08:24.260
Right.

08:24.260 --> 08:26.240
What you are describing is stage two.

08:26.440 --> 08:28.760
Okay. So let's start at stage one, just to set the baseline.

08:28.920 --> 08:40.120
Right. Stage one is total separation. The computer and the heater are distinct entities separated by miles, and the heat from the computer is entirely vented into the atmosphere.

08:40.120 --> 08:44.920
Pure waste. That is the status quo for the vast majority of global compute right now.

08:45.100 --> 08:48.560
Exactly. Then we reach stage two, waste heat recovery.

08:48.560 --> 08:56.940
This is where you grab the heat exchanger onto a data center, hook it up to a municipal water loop, and capture that thermal exhaust to heat a neighborhood.

08:57.140 --> 09:01.280
Which, on the surface, sounds fantastic. You're capturing the garbage and recycling it.

09:01.300 --> 09:09.400
It is undeniably better than stage one. But fliction argues it is fundamentally insufficient because it preserves the split optimization domains.

09:09.500 --> 09:10.300
What do you mean by that?

09:10.300 --> 09:17.740
Think about the physical and institutional realities. The data center operator is still optimizing only for computational throughput.

09:17.740 --> 09:20.660
Their primary concern is keeping their servers online.

09:21.100 --> 09:25.240
The municipal building manager is still optimizing only for thermal comfort.

09:25.960 --> 09:30.980
You simply attach a secondary output pipe onto a primary system that was designed without it.

09:31.260 --> 09:35.960
I see. So if it's unseasonably warm outside, the town doesn't need heat.

09:36.540 --> 09:38.320
Does the data center stop computing?

09:38.920 --> 09:42.740
No, of course not. They just switch back to venting the heat into the air.

09:42.740 --> 09:48.360
And if there's a blizzard, but the data center doesn't happen to have a lot of trapping that day, the town still freezes.

09:48.720 --> 09:52.280
Precisely. The institutional boundaries remain completely intact.

09:52.900 --> 09:55.840
The core architecture of both systems hasn't changed.

09:56.200 --> 09:59.720
They are just sort of awkwardly holding hands across a property line.

09:59.960 --> 10:02.380
That's a great image. Okay, so what's the real solution then?

10:02.760 --> 10:04.420
That brings us to the core of the proposal.

10:05.160 --> 10:07.760
Stage three. Stage three is true. Native integration.

10:07.960 --> 10:09.120
Native integration. Okay.

10:09.120 --> 10:12.800
This is where the computer and the heater are the exact same physical object.

10:12.940 --> 10:13.280
Wow.

10:13.560 --> 10:26.940
They are co-located precisely where the heat demand is, and they are governed by a single shared scheduler that treats computational throughput and thermal output as simultaneous primary outputs of the exact same physical process.

10:27.180 --> 10:37.240
So instead of the computer being in a warehouse three miles away sending hot water through a leaky pipe, the computer is the actual metal box radiating heat in my living room.

10:37.240 --> 10:39.000
Yes. Exactly.

10:39.020 --> 10:41.060
That is, I mean, that's a massive shift.

10:41.200 --> 10:47.960
It is. And to understand why we are stuck in stage one and occasionally stage two, we really have to look backward.

10:48.840 --> 10:52.860
The separation of heat and compute is not dictated by the laws of thermodynamics.

10:53.240 --> 10:54.260
It's not physics.

10:54.260 --> 11:07.040
No, it is dictated by institutional friction. Electrical grids, telecommunications, silicon manufacturing, and residential HVAC systems all evolved as completely separate sectors over the last century.

11:07.380 --> 11:08.580
They're totally different worlds.

11:08.840 --> 11:13.440
Right. They have distinct capital structures, different ownership models, and entirely different regulatory codes.

11:13.440 --> 11:17.420
It's a bureaucratic artifact. I can just imagine a city planner, right?

11:17.880 --> 11:24.500
They have a highly detailed zoning code for a residential furnace and a highly detailed zoning code for a commercial server farm.

11:24.720 --> 11:25.040
Yes.

11:25.420 --> 11:32.060
But if you try to hand them a blueprint for an appliance that does both, their system just throws an error. There is no box to check.

11:32.060 --> 11:45.320
Exactly. When computation was a tiny sliver of global energy use and heating fuel was incredibly cheap, throwing away the thermal exhaust from early mainframes wasn't an economic disaster.

11:45.440 --> 11:46.740
Right. It was just a rounding error.

11:47.020 --> 11:53.400
Right. It certainly wasn't worth the organizational headache of trying to merge the IT industry with the HVAC industry.

11:53.540 --> 11:54.140
But today?

11:54.140 --> 12:04.320
Today, the energy demand of global computation is scaling exponentially, while the geopolitical and environmental cost of residential heating is reaching an absolute breaking point.

12:05.080 --> 12:08.420
We can no longer afford to treat these as non-overlapping domains.

12:08.420 --> 12:16.420
Okay. So if stage two, the pipe between the buildings, isn't the answer, what does this stage three true integration look like conceptually?

12:16.720 --> 12:24.080
Because Flixian argues that to find the blueprint, we need to stop looking at human industrial history and start looking at arboreal biology.

12:24.340 --> 12:26.160
Yes. We need to look at forests.

12:26.320 --> 12:29.220
Which is such a fascinating pivot in an engineering paper.

12:29.260 --> 12:33.120
It really is. This is where the paper introduces the term that anchors the whole theory.

12:34.080 --> 12:34.560
Xylomorphic.

12:34.560 --> 12:40.860
Xylomorphic. Xylo meaning wood, morphic meaning shape or form. So taking the structural form of a tree.

12:41.160 --> 12:41.600
Exactly.

12:41.880 --> 12:42.100
Yeah.

12:42.320 --> 12:49.560
It designates a design philosophy derived directly from the structural and functional organization of trees and forest systems.

12:49.700 --> 12:50.860
Okay. Break that down for us.

12:50.920 --> 13:00.440
The fundamental insight here is that nature does not use the separated substrate model. A tree does not optimize for a single output.

13:00.780 --> 13:01.040
Right.

13:01.040 --> 13:12.720
The physical material of a tree, the bark, the root cystae, the cell walls, the vascular bundles, performs multiple critical tasks simultaneously within the exact same physical space.

13:12.820 --> 13:15.180
So it's multitasking on a cellular level.

13:15.400 --> 13:17.960
Absolutely. It provides mechanical structure against gravity.

13:18.080 --> 13:20.880
It provides hydraulic transport to move water and nutrients.

13:21.080 --> 13:23.420
It provides thermal regulation for its cells.

13:23.800 --> 13:25.240
It serves as a biological habitat.

13:25.440 --> 13:28.300
And all of these functions are happening in the exact same physical mass.

13:28.300 --> 13:28.700
Exactly.

13:28.920 --> 13:34.940
It makes me think of the difference between a terribly equipped kitchen and a perfectly engineered multi-tool.

13:35.160 --> 13:35.980
Oh, I like that.

13:36.200 --> 13:41.420
Yeah. Human engineering is like a kitchen drawer full of hyper-specific single-use gadgets.

13:41.940 --> 13:45.700
You know, you've got an avocado slicer, a strawberry huller, a garlic press.

13:45.760 --> 13:47.100
Right. The things you use once a year.

13:47.620 --> 13:50.520
Exactly. Every tool does exactly one thing.

13:50.520 --> 13:56.000
And when you aren't doing that specific task, the tool just sits there consuming space and resources.

13:56.340 --> 13:57.740
A power plant makes electricity.

13:58.420 --> 13:59.480
A furnace makes heat.

13:59.680 --> 14:02.660
Right. But nature operates like a multi-tool.

14:02.960 --> 14:10.600
Every edge, every flat surface, every handle serves three or four purposes simultaneously depending on the context of the environment.

14:10.600 --> 14:12.140
That's a great conceptual bridge.

14:12.340 --> 14:12.460
Yes.

14:12.600 --> 14:14.820
We are obsessed with the single output principle.

14:15.460 --> 14:21.720
We maximize performance on a single metric and brutally marginalize everything else as a constraint to be minimized.

14:21.920 --> 14:24.900
But the xylomorphic premise inverts this framing.

14:25.140 --> 14:25.560
It does.

14:26.320 --> 14:30.580
In a tree, the metabolic heat produced by cellular respiration isn't wasted.

14:31.260 --> 14:35.640
It actually participates in the thermal gradient that drives water transport up the xylem.

14:35.820 --> 14:36.340
Wow.

14:36.340 --> 14:41.100
The mechanical stress of wind pushing against the trunk isn't just a threat to be mitigated.

14:41.260 --> 14:45.780
It acts as a physical signal that regulates where the tree allocates its growth hormones.

14:45.900 --> 14:46.680
That's incredible.

14:46.880 --> 14:47.920
The quote flicks you directly.

14:48.220 --> 14:49.840
Nothing that can be used is discarded.

14:50.260 --> 14:59.000
So if we apply that biological philosophy back to our problem, we know physically that computation must produce heat.

14:59.200 --> 15:04.780
Right. Every time a microchip flips a bit, thermodynamics demands a tiny release of thermal energy.

15:04.780 --> 15:09.260
We also know that cold climates absolutely require heat to sustain human life.

15:09.440 --> 15:09.620
Yes.

15:09.900 --> 15:19.140
So instead of maximizing computation and treating the heat as this costly enemy, we should design a system that maximizes the sum of the useful outputs.

15:19.280 --> 15:20.100
The sum of the utility.

15:20.360 --> 15:20.540
Yeah.

15:20.620 --> 15:30.300
Why not build a system where the total societal value is the combination of the cognitive work done by the processor plus the thermal comfort provided to the human?

15:30.300 --> 15:35.980
I love the philosophy. The arboreal analogy is beautiful. But we don't live in a metaphorical forest.

15:36.140 --> 15:36.720
No, we don't.

15:36.800 --> 15:45.280
We live in a world of concrete, asphalt, aging power grids, and suburban sprawl. How do we actually operationalize a xylomorphic network?

15:45.480 --> 15:46.280
That's the big question.

15:46.380 --> 15:57.060
Right. Because if the computer needs to be in my living room, do we have to tear up every street in North America to lay down some kind of new thermodynamic distribution network? Because if so, this idea is dead on arrival.

15:57.060 --> 16:03.220
And this is exactly where fliction moves from theory to incredibly pragmatic engineering.

16:03.500 --> 16:03.720
Okay.

16:04.140 --> 16:13.820
The paper argues that the infrastructure for Stage 3 already exists. It is already buried under our streets and hanging from our utility poles. We don't need a new network.

16:13.960 --> 16:14.560
We don't.

16:14.620 --> 16:17.880
No. We just need to repurpose the fiber optic internet.

16:18.240 --> 16:22.040
The internet cables. The same fiber that streams movies to my TV.

16:22.040 --> 16:29.900
Yes. Look at a topology of the fiber optic network. It is entirely different from the centralized topology of a traditional power grid.

16:30.100 --> 16:30.580
How so?

16:31.000 --> 16:45.160
Well, on a power grid, one massive power plant radiates electricity outward to passive consumers. But fiber optics are distributed. They run from major trunks into neighborhood street-level routers and terminate directly inside individual homes.

16:45.280 --> 16:45.720
Ugh.

16:45.720 --> 16:50.140
The physical layout of the internet tracks the distribution of human populations almost perfectly.

16:50.460 --> 16:53.700
And because it tracks human populations, it perfectly maps the heat demand field.

16:53.780 --> 16:57.080
Exactly. Where there are humans, there is a requirement for warmth.

16:57.160 --> 16:57.960
Oh, it's so elegant.

16:58.360 --> 17:03.060
The existing fiber infrastructure is already a high-bandwidth, low-latency nervous system.

17:03.160 --> 17:03.360
Yeah.

17:03.360 --> 17:15.260
From the perspective of the network layer, a packet of data delivering a 4K movie and a packet of data delivering a complex matrix multiplication job for an AI are structurally identical.

17:15.420 --> 17:22.840
Right. They both just consume bandwidth. They both require routing. The fiber cable entering your home doesn't care what the math is for.

17:22.840 --> 17:29.640
Precisely. So the geographic addressing is already solved. Every home that needs heat already has an IP address.

17:29.860 --> 17:34.800
The paper argues that your household IP address effectively functions as a thermal address, right?

17:34.900 --> 17:43.200
Yes. It is a specific geographic coordinate with a known fiber connection, a known historical heating profile, and a resident GPU heater node.

17:43.380 --> 17:52.240
Let's dig into the hardware there. What exactly is a GPU heater node? Because if I put a standard server rack in my living room, it's going to sound like a jet engine and blow dust everywhere.

17:52.240 --> 18:02.560
So absolutely. A GPU heater node is not a standard server. It's a self-contained computational appliance designed specifically for residential or light commercial integration.

18:03.000 --> 18:15.440
Unlike a traditional PC or server that uses high RPM fans to violently expel heat away from itself, this node is designed to capture and direct its thermal output deliberately into the building's thermal envelope.

18:15.620 --> 18:17.040
So it's plumbed into the house.

18:17.040 --> 18:22.100
Yeah. Perhaps tied directly into a hydronic radiant floor system or a forced air duct.

18:22.240 --> 18:30.300
It sits in your home, performs highly valuable computation for the global network, and provides space heating as an intrinsic co-product.

18:30.620 --> 18:36.640
Okay. So let's say a pharmaceutical company needs to run a massive simulation to fold proteins for a new drug.

18:36.820 --> 18:37.480
Great example.

18:37.740 --> 18:45.160
Instead of sending that job to a hyperscale data center in the desert, the network scheduler chops that massive job into smaller pieces,

18:45.160 --> 18:50.460
routes one piece through the fiber optic network directly into my house to my GPU heater node.

18:50.540 --> 18:50.840
Right.

18:50.840 --> 18:58.740
The node does the math, sends the results back over the fiber, and the thermal energy generated by doing that math radiates into my living room.

18:58.980 --> 19:00.260
That is the exact mechanism.

19:00.420 --> 19:00.580
Yeah.

19:00.840 --> 19:06.900
But it requires an incredibly sophisticated network scheduler to prevent catastrophic physical outcomes.

19:06.900 --> 19:10.760
Well, like turning your house into a sauna in the middle of a July heat wave.

19:10.920 --> 19:13.820
Oh. Right. That would be bad.

19:14.020 --> 19:14.580
Very bad.

19:14.860 --> 19:15.200
Ah.

19:15.500 --> 19:20.080
The scheduling algorithm fliction outlines is based on a strict constraint protocol.

19:20.340 --> 19:21.340
How does that work?

19:21.340 --> 19:26.100
Let's say C sub I is the computation allocated to your specific home node.

19:26.620 --> 19:37.520
The scheduler assigns C sub I based on the lesser of two variables, the global network's demand for computational power or your specific home's immediate demand for heat.

19:37.800 --> 19:39.960
Wait, the lesser, so it acts like a bottleneck.

19:40.020 --> 19:41.720
Exactly, a thermal bottleneck.

19:41.720 --> 19:48.960
So if the network is slammed with work, but my living room thermostat is reading 72 degrees, the scheduler skips my house.

19:49.340 --> 19:53.100
It refuses to send the job because my thermal constraint is the bottleneck.

19:53.480 --> 19:55.380
Your physical comfort is the hard ceiling.

19:56.020 --> 19:58.220
The scheduler interfaces with your local thermostat.

19:58.660 --> 20:04.100
If you require heat and the network has queued work, the node receives the job and spins up.

20:04.220 --> 20:06.180
I can already see the engineering nightmare here, though.

20:06.260 --> 20:07.120
What happens in the summer?

20:07.620 --> 20:08.360
Ah, yes.

20:08.720 --> 20:09.660
The summer problem.

20:09.900 --> 20:10.160
Right.

20:10.160 --> 20:16.000
If I live in a climate where I don't turn my heat on for five months, does my node just get disconnected from the internet?

20:16.580 --> 20:20.300
Did the tech companies just lose all that computing power for half the year?

20:20.740 --> 20:24.820
This is a massive hardware hurdle that Flixing details extensively.

20:25.140 --> 20:28.260
It's called the absolute necessity of the idle state.

20:28.460 --> 20:29.260
The idle state.

20:29.520 --> 20:35.920
You obviously cannot run these nodes in the summer unless you want to fight your own air conditioning, which ruins the efficiency gains.

20:36.120 --> 20:36.440
Hmm.

20:36.900 --> 20:39.960
But you also can't completely power the node down.

20:39.960 --> 20:41.200
Because it's part of a network.

20:41.540 --> 20:41.980
Exactly.

20:42.760 --> 20:48.600
It needs to maintain its memory state, stay cryptographically synced with the network, and be ready to receive patches.

20:49.280 --> 20:53.900
Therefore, the silicon architecture needs an unprecedented dynamic power range.

20:54.020 --> 20:57.080
Meaning it has to operate at wildly different voltage level.

20:57.080 --> 21:05.640
It needs to be capable of sitting in a deep, microscopic idle state, drawing single-digit watts, producing effectively zero ambient heat just staying awake.

21:05.720 --> 21:06.860
Barely sipping power.

21:07.220 --> 21:07.460
Right.

21:07.840 --> 21:20.900
But the moment a cold front rolls in and your thermostat clicks on, the node needs to be able to instantly ramp up to hundreds or even thousands of watts to process a backlog of network jobs while rapidly heating your home.

21:20.900 --> 21:22.660
And current ships can't do that.

21:22.820 --> 21:23.560
Not efficiently.

21:24.380 --> 21:28.580
Current ships are not optimized for that kind of extreme elastic scaling.

21:28.780 --> 21:30.300
They like to run hot and steady.

21:30.540 --> 21:30.820
Okay.

21:30.940 --> 21:33.880
I follow the logic, but let me put on my skeptical homeowner hat.

21:34.020 --> 21:34.480
Put it on.

21:34.480 --> 21:36.900
Or better yet, my city energy manager hat.

21:37.420 --> 21:40.480
Right now, the gold standard for residential heating is the heat pump.

21:41.000 --> 21:44.180
And heat pumps are wildly famously efficient.

21:44.440 --> 21:44.860
They are.

21:45.080 --> 21:53.260
Depending on the outdoor temperature, a heat pump gives you maybe three to five units of heat for every one unit of electricity you feed it.

21:53.300 --> 21:58.600
Because it achieves that by moving ambient heat from outside to inside rather than generating it from scratch.

21:58.600 --> 22:05.420
Right. Now, a GPU, or any electrical resistance heater, gives you exactly one unit of heat for one unit of electricity.

22:05.560 --> 22:06.560
It's a one-to-one ratio.

22:06.920 --> 22:13.260
Exactly. So on a strictly thermal basis, my heat pump is three to five times better at warming my house than a GPU.

22:13.920 --> 22:18.540
Why would I want to regress to a system that is fundamentally less efficient at its primary job?

22:18.860 --> 22:23.480
It is the first question any mechanical engineer will ask when presented with this paper.

22:23.480 --> 22:30.880
And if you limit your perspective strictly to the thermal coefficient of performance, the COP and, the heat pump wins effortlessly.

22:31.300 --> 22:32.020
So why do it?

22:32.340 --> 22:35.440
Because Flicksheen argues that this metric is structurally blind.

22:36.400 --> 22:40.420
It ignores the core xylomorphic premise, total output efficiency.

22:41.000 --> 22:43.480
Break down the math of total output efficiency for me.

22:43.660 --> 22:46.000
A heat pump performs zero computation.

22:46.500 --> 22:48.720
Its functional output is exclusively thermal.

22:48.900 --> 22:49.180
Right.

22:49.180 --> 22:55.140
The GPU heater node produces one unit of heat plus the economic and functional value of the computation it performed.

22:55.620 --> 22:57.180
Let's call it economic value V subcompute.

22:57.820 --> 22:58.660
V subcompute.

22:58.740 --> 23:00.960
We know that V subcompute is massive.

23:01.660 --> 23:05.180
The AI training industry is constrained by compute, not capital.

23:05.480 --> 23:07.920
They will pay anything for processing power right now.

23:08.180 --> 23:08.740
Exactly.

23:08.740 --> 23:19.640
If you measure the efficiency of the GPU node by combining the thermal comfort it provided with the market value of the data processed, its total societal efficiency dwarfs the heat pump.

23:19.860 --> 23:20.340
Wow.

23:20.520 --> 23:27.380
You are solving two highly expensive problems warming a human and training a neural network with a single wad of electricity.

23:28.020 --> 23:29.680
The heat pump only solves one.

23:30.080 --> 23:33.600
Going back to our analogy, the heat pump is a single-use kitchen gadget.

23:33.960 --> 23:34.220
Right.

23:34.220 --> 23:36.580
The GPU node is the multi-tool.

23:37.080 --> 23:43.980
It might not be the absolute best dedicated heater in the world, but it's paying my utility bill by doing a second job at the same time.

23:44.140 --> 23:44.580
Precisely.

23:45.080 --> 23:46.860
Furthermore, the heat pumps are always better.

23:47.620 --> 23:49.740
Argument has a severe physical vulnerability.

23:50.140 --> 23:50.980
The cold weather limit.

23:51.260 --> 23:51.560
Yes.

23:52.180 --> 23:56.620
That magical 3 to 1 efficiency ratio depends entirely on the outdoor ambient temperature.

23:57.040 --> 24:00.360
As the weather gets colder, there's less ambient heat for the pump to extract.

24:00.360 --> 24:00.880
Right.

24:00.940 --> 24:07.860
By the time you hit extreme cold snaps, say, dropping below minus 15 degrees Celsius, the efficiency of an air source heat pump just collapses.

24:08.340 --> 24:19.300
The compressor works harder, it frosts over, and eventually it drops down to a 1 to 1 efficiency ratio, relying on internal electric resistance coils just to keep the pipes from freezing.

24:19.640 --> 24:19.880
Right.

24:19.960 --> 24:20.780
Those backup strips.

24:21.220 --> 24:28.180
So in the absolute worst weather, when the power grid is under maximum stress, the heat pump loses its superpower anyway.

24:28.180 --> 24:31.420
But the GPU heater node is thermally invariant.

24:31.560 --> 24:32.580
Thermally invariant.

24:32.640 --> 24:33.660
What does that mean in practice?

24:34.180 --> 24:38.300
It does not care if it is negative 30 degrees outside or positive 10.

24:39.020 --> 24:44.120
One watt of electricity into a semiconductor will always produce one watt of heat.

24:44.280 --> 24:44.720
Ah.

24:45.420 --> 24:57.240
So precisely when the grid is most vulnerable and the climate is most punishing, the GPU is thermally identical to the struggling heat pump, with the massive added benefit of producing highly valuable computational work.

24:57.240 --> 25:00.480
So Fliction isn't suggesting we go rip the heat pumps out of our walls?

25:00.620 --> 25:01.100
Not at all.

25:01.400 --> 25:04.240
The idealized stage 3 integration is a hybrid ecosystem.

25:04.680 --> 25:10.940
A home equipped with an air source heat pump, a GPU heater node, and thermal storage like a highly insulated water buffer tank.

25:11.160 --> 25:12.940
How does that ecosystem balance itself?

25:13.080 --> 25:14.200
Like, who takes the lead?

25:14.500 --> 25:16.160
It becomes a thermodynamics symphony.

25:16.240 --> 25:17.160
I love that phrase.

25:17.160 --> 25:21.760
During mild winter days, the heat pump does the heavy lifting because its cop is high.

25:22.300 --> 25:27.520
The GPU node provides a steady baseline of heat while processing steady state network jobs.

25:27.640 --> 25:28.620
Okay, that makes sense.

25:28.920 --> 25:38.920
But what happens at 3 a.m. when the network scheduler urgently needs a massive batch of computation processed, but you are asleep and your house is already warm?

25:38.920 --> 25:45.920
The GPU fires up to maximum capacity and dumps all that excess thermal energy into the water buffer tank.

25:46.000 --> 25:46.600
Oh, smart.

25:46.720 --> 25:54.100
Then, at 7 a.m. when you wake up and need the house heated, but the network traffic has died down, your home draws the heat out of the water tank.

25:54.420 --> 26:02.640
The house itself becomes a biological cell, storing energy, processing data, managing its own thermal equilibrium based on external environmental inputs.

26:02.640 --> 26:11.020
Exactly. And to prove this isn't merely academic poetry, segment four of the paper grounds this in a highly specific, mathematically rigorous thought experiment.

26:11.260 --> 26:12.840
I love a good stress test. Where are we going?

26:13.040 --> 26:19.100
We are going to a hypothetical 100-home residential subdivision in New Brunswick, Canada, in the middle of January.

26:19.540 --> 26:24.080
Why New Brunswick? Why not somewhere with massive data centers like Virginia or Texas?

26:24.500 --> 26:27.380
Because New Brunswick is the perfect crucible for this technology.

26:27.380 --> 26:38.300
First, the climate is punishing. Average January temperatures hover between negative 10 and negative 15 degrees Celsius, with regular dips well below negative 20.

26:38.560 --> 26:40.080
That is painfully cold.

26:40.380 --> 26:46.440
Second, they have an established, robust fiber-optic infrastructure penetrating deep into residential areas.

26:46.540 --> 26:47.500
So the network is there.

26:47.620 --> 26:53.360
Yes. And third, the local population historically bears a very high home heating cost relative to income.

26:53.960 --> 26:57.180
Thermal energy is a painful, expensive necessity there.

26:57.180 --> 27:03.720
Okay, so we have 100 freezing homes. What is the baseline physics here? How much energy does it take to keep these houses warm?

27:04.280 --> 27:09.640
Fliction models a standard 150-square-meter detached house, roughly 1,600 square feet.

27:10.180 --> 27:17.900
During a typical January cold snap, a house that's size bleeds heat so fast that it requires roughly 5 to 8 kilowatts of continuous thermal power,

27:18.320 --> 27:21.320
just to maintain a safe interior temperature of 20 degrees Celsius.

27:21.320 --> 27:31.560
Five to 8 kilowatts running continuously, multiply that by 100 homes, you're talking about a massive constant drain on the provincial grid just to fight the weather.

27:31.780 --> 27:36.380
Right. So if Fliction models two interventions, scenario A is a light deployment.

27:36.540 --> 27:38.020
Okay, what does light deployment look like?

27:38.240 --> 27:41.380
Imagine only 40 out of the 100 homes opt into the program.

27:41.380 --> 27:46.200
Each of those 40 homes receives a modest 400-watt GPU node.

27:46.860 --> 27:54.440
To visualize that, it's roughly the power draw of a high-end gaming PC or a standard space heater you plug in under your desk, right?

27:54.460 --> 27:55.080
Yes, exactly.

27:55.240 --> 27:59.020
Okay, so 40 homes have the equivalent of a heavy-duty PC running jobs in the corner.

27:59.420 --> 28:01.340
Does that actually dent the winter cold?

28:01.340 --> 28:03.540
Thermally, the impact is marginal.

28:04.260 --> 28:10.860
40 nodes operating continuously at 400 watts yield 16 kilowatts of continuous thermal output for the neighborhood.

28:11.260 --> 28:18.340
Against that massive baseline demand you mentioned, that only covers about 2% to 3% of the subdivision's total heating requirements.

28:19.040 --> 28:19.260
Right.

28:19.500 --> 28:22.380
Which honestly feels like a rounding error. It doesn't sound like a revolution.

28:22.560 --> 28:24.140
It's a rounding error thermally, absolutely.

28:24.320 --> 28:27.400
But look at the economics of total output efficiency.

28:27.400 --> 28:32.020
Those nodes aren't just radiating heat. They are processing global network jobs.

28:32.980 --> 28:42.060
Flixion estimates that 16 kilowatts of continuous computational dispatch, using incredibly conservative cloud compute pricing of around 5 cents per kilowatt hour,

28:42.540 --> 28:46.460
generates roughly $575 a month in direct compute revenue.

28:46.720 --> 28:50.640
Wait, almost $600 a month just from 40 gaming PCs?

28:50.940 --> 28:51.180
Yes.

28:51.180 --> 29:00.620
Distributed among the 40 participating households, each home is natively earning about $14 a month in network compensation, while getting that 2% of their heat for free.

29:01.060 --> 29:02.920
Okay, I mean, $14 won't pay the mortgage?

29:03.280 --> 29:03.600
No.

29:03.880 --> 29:09.900
But the psychological shift is profound. You are literally being paid to operate a heater inside your own home.

29:10.180 --> 29:12.420
The utility model is entirely inverted.

29:12.420 --> 29:18.700
And that's just the baseline. To see the civilizational potential, Flixion models scenario B, a dense deployment.

29:18.900 --> 29:20.400
Bring on the dense deployment.

29:20.400 --> 29:26.880
Imagine all 100 homes are equipped not with a 400-watt box, but with a dedicated 2-kilowatt rack.

29:27.280 --> 29:32.680
This is a specialized appliance, perhaps the size of a MIDI fridge, integrated directly into the basement utility room.

29:32.740 --> 29:35.260
A 2-kilowatt rack in every single home.

29:35.500 --> 29:44.380
Now, the network scheduler cannot physically run all 100 nodes at 100% capacity continuously without overheating the homes during milder days.

29:44.460 --> 29:46.260
Right. Nobody wants to sweat in January.

29:46.260 --> 29:54.540
So Flixion assumes a realistic dispatch rate, where the scheduler utilizes about 60% of that total capacity at any given time.

29:54.620 --> 30:00.000
Let me do the math on that. 60% of 100 homes is 60 active nodes, each pulling 2-kilowatts.

30:00.300 --> 30:03.500
That's 120-kilowatts of continuous thermal output.

30:03.680 --> 30:06.340
Which fundamentally alters the neighborhood's energy profile.

30:06.340 --> 30:15.300
At 120 kilowatts, those computational nodes are natively supplying between 15% and 24% of the entire subdivision's thermal demand.

30:15.420 --> 30:22.480
Wow. Nearly a quarter of the neighborhood's heating needs generated purely as a physical byproduct of the internet doing its daily chores.

30:22.700 --> 30:29.620
It is staggering. A 20% reduction in residential heating demand across a severe cold climate is a civilization-scale resource.

30:29.720 --> 30:30.400
It really is.

30:30.400 --> 30:35.460
Extrapolate that 100-home model across every winter city in North America, Northern Europe, and Asia.

30:35.760 --> 30:42.200
You're talking about reclaiming millions of megawatt hours of energy that is currently being vented into the sky by commercial data centers.

30:42.480 --> 30:45.420
But this brings up a glaring logistical vulnerability.

30:45.680 --> 30:46.400
Okay, what's that?

30:46.520 --> 30:52.060
What kind of computation are we actually sending to a residential basement in New Brunswick?

30:52.060 --> 31:00.220
I assume you can't run the back-end infrastructure for a high-frequency trading firm in Manhattan from a decentralized node in Canada.

31:00.380 --> 31:01.420
No, you definitely can't.

31:01.480 --> 31:06.240
Right, because the latency, the time it takes the signal to travel back and forth would just ruin the trades.

31:06.480 --> 31:07.300
And what about Disney?

31:08.080 --> 31:16.760
They aren't going to send unreleased proprietary Pixar rendering files to a random guy's basement where he could potentially intercept the data.

31:17.040 --> 31:18.520
Security is a huge issue.

31:18.520 --> 31:21.660
So how does the network manage the reality of time and security?

31:22.060 --> 31:29.300
It is the defining constraint of the system, and Fliction addresses it through a framework called workload partitioning.

31:29.500 --> 31:30.860
Workload partitioning.

31:31.080 --> 31:35.960
Not all digital tasks are created equal, and more importantly, they are not all relocatable.

31:36.680 --> 31:40.040
The paper categorizes global computation into four distinct pools.

31:40.620 --> 31:42.140
The first is D-sublatency.

31:42.240 --> 31:44.340
Okay, latency-sensitive workloads.

31:44.780 --> 31:45.020
Right.

31:45.020 --> 31:50.680
Real-time financial trading, multiplayer gaming servers, interactive web applications.

31:50.680 --> 31:52.220
You are absolutely right.

31:52.320 --> 31:53.460
These cannot be moved.

31:53.880 --> 31:58.560
They must physically reside close to the end user to minimize the speed of light delay.

31:58.920 --> 32:00.620
So Wall Street stays on Wall Street?

32:00.900 --> 32:01.300
Exactly.

32:01.800 --> 32:03.480
The second pool is D-subsecure.

32:04.040 --> 32:09.140
Workloads bound by extreme data sovereignty, legal compliance, or military secrecy.

32:09.440 --> 32:12.540
Like patient medical records or classified defense simulations?

32:12.820 --> 32:14.900
Or proprietary corporate algorithms, yes.

32:14.900 --> 32:20.460
You cannot route this data to unsecured residential nodes, regardless of how good the encryption is.

32:20.940 --> 32:24.100
These must remain in fortified, centralized data centers.

32:24.240 --> 32:24.920
Makes total sense.

32:24.980 --> 32:29.800
So if we exclude the fast stuff and the secret stuff, what is actually left to send to the houses?

32:30.100 --> 32:34.260
The vast majority of the future compute demand, actually, which falls into the third and fourth pools.

32:34.760 --> 32:37.640
The third is D-subbatch, or batch workloads.

32:37.760 --> 32:38.380
Batch workloads?

32:38.480 --> 32:38.960
What are those?

32:38.960 --> 32:43.120
These are massive, computationally heavy jobs that are completely agnostic to latency.

32:43.600 --> 32:48.680
They take hours, days, or weeks to run, and the user does not care about millisecond response times.

32:48.800 --> 32:51.440
Oh, like training large language models for AI.

32:52.000 --> 32:52.440
Exactly.

32:53.080 --> 32:58.880
Processing massive genomic sequencing data sets, running global climate simulations.

32:59.100 --> 32:59.380
Ah.

33:00.000 --> 33:02.920
They don't care when the math gets done, so long as it gets done eventually.

33:02.980 --> 33:03.400
Exactly.

33:03.580 --> 33:08.620
They are fundamentally flexible in time, which means they are flexible in location.

33:08.620 --> 33:10.160
Okay, I see where this is going.

33:10.500 --> 33:31.960
If a research university needs a massive simulation run by Friday, the network scheduler can hold the job on Monday, look at the meteorological forecast, see that a massive blizzard is hitting New Brunswick on Tuesday night, and cue the entire batch workload to blast out to those hundred homes precisely when their thermostats demand maximum heat.

33:32.040 --> 33:32.940
It's like doing laundry.

33:33.560 --> 33:34.000
How do you mean?

33:34.000 --> 33:42.060
Well, if you spill coffee on your shirt and you have a date in an hour, you have to wash and dry that shirt right now, regardless of how expensive peak electricity is.

33:42.660 --> 33:44.240
That's a latency-sensitive workload.

33:44.620 --> 33:47.800
But if you're washing your bedsheets and towels, you don't care when they get done.

33:47.880 --> 33:54.280
You can throw them in the machine and set a timer so it runs at 3 a.m. on a Sunday when utility rates are rock bottom.

33:54.860 --> 33:55.900
That's a batch workload.

33:55.900 --> 34:00.860
You are saying the network can treat global weather patterns as a scheduling parameter.

34:01.080 --> 34:02.680
Climate as a scheduling parameter.

34:02.840 --> 34:04.720
It's a beautiful distillation of the concept.

34:05.620 --> 34:08.020
And it's supplemented by the fourth pool, B-subthermal.

34:08.580 --> 34:13.120
These are workloads explicitly engineered for distributed variable environments.

34:13.700 --> 34:15.460
Cryptographic hashing for blockchains.

34:16.040 --> 34:17.660
Lightweight edge AI inference.

34:18.020 --> 34:23.660
Jobs that can be paused and resumed seamlessly based on the physical thermal constraints of the host node.

34:23.660 --> 34:32.620
Exactly. Between D-subbatch and D-subthermal, there is a practically infinite well of relocatable computation to keep these homes warm.

34:32.880 --> 34:33.880
Okay, so I'm with you.

34:33.960 --> 34:34.700
We've solved the logic.

34:34.840 --> 34:35.560
We've solved the logistics.

34:35.760 --> 34:36.700
We've solved the topology.

34:37.380 --> 34:42.700
Stage 3 integration, putting a computer and a heater in the same box, is a brilliant infrastructural pivot.

34:42.820 --> 34:43.200
That is.

34:43.320 --> 34:49.460
But when we discussed the outline for this deep dive beforehand, you warned me that stage 3 was just the warm-up act.

34:49.820 --> 34:50.600
Oh, it really is.

34:50.600 --> 34:57.780
Because if my GPU is heating the room, it's still fundamentally a separate processor chip doing math and a separate fan blowing air.

34:58.360 --> 35:03.660
Haven't we just created a miniature, localized version of the separated substrate inside the box?

35:03.700 --> 35:06.180
That is the exact intellectual leapflexion makes.

35:06.320 --> 35:06.580
Okay.

35:06.580 --> 35:12.460
Stage 3 places the two systems in the same chassis, but they remain physically distinct processes.

35:13.120 --> 35:15.300
The silicon calculates, the metal radiates.

35:15.680 --> 35:20.160
Segment 5 of the paper introduces stage 4, physical co-computation.

35:20.160 --> 35:21.660
Physical co-computation.

35:21.720 --> 35:26.000
This is where the physics of the paper go from innovative to completely radical.

35:26.160 --> 35:27.380
Explain stage 4 to me.

35:27.560 --> 35:28.080
What is it?

35:28.160 --> 35:35.560
In a stage 4 node, the thermal, fluidic, and optical components are no longer just cooling mechanisms attached to a digital chip.

35:35.800 --> 35:39.760
The physical movement and dynamics of those very materials are the computation.

35:40.120 --> 35:40.800
Wait, what?

35:40.800 --> 35:45.920
The boundary between the thermodynamic process and the mathematical process dissolves entirely.

35:45.920 --> 35:48.220
Okay, I'm going to need you to ELI-5 that.

35:48.420 --> 35:49.520
Explain it like I'm 5.

35:49.640 --> 35:50.440
I know, it sounds crazy.

35:50.760 --> 36:00.760
Because my brain is rejecting the concept that a pipe full of water or a chunk of metal can do math without a microchip, I need the actual physical mechanisms.

36:00.760 --> 36:03.860
Let's walk through the exact examples Flixion provides.

36:04.020 --> 36:07.060
They rely on established, verifiable physics.

36:07.380 --> 36:08.760
Let's start with fluid dynamics.

36:08.940 --> 36:09.640
Okay, water.

36:09.880 --> 36:10.080
Right.

36:10.640 --> 36:18.660
Imagine a stage 4 node containing a pressurized fluid network, a complex physical maze of pipes, valves, and microscopic channels.

36:19.160 --> 36:24.560
In a traditional server, you pump water through pipes purely to absorb heat from the processor.

36:24.720 --> 36:25.740
Right, water tooling.

36:25.740 --> 36:31.860
But in fluid logic, the geometry of the plate network is deliberately designed to mimic a mathematical graph.

36:32.060 --> 36:33.060
How does that solve anything?

36:33.480 --> 36:41.660
Well, if you want to solve a highly complex routing optimization problem, say, finding the most efficient way for a delivery company to wrap 10,000 packages,

36:42.000 --> 36:45.680
you encode the parameters of the problem into the physical pressure valves of the network.

36:45.880 --> 36:46.120
Okay.

36:46.380 --> 36:48.980
Then, you simply force fluid through the maze.

36:49.520 --> 36:52.500
The physical fluid naturally follows the path of least resistance.

36:52.500 --> 37:00.700
By placing pressure sensors at the output ports, you literally read the fluid's physical state to find the mathematical answer.

37:00.820 --> 37:01.020
Wait.

37:01.460 --> 37:05.160
The water solves the maze just by being forced through it.

37:05.520 --> 37:09.580
The physical pressure of the fluid doing what it naturally wants to do is the calculation.

37:09.820 --> 37:10.080
Yes.

37:10.480 --> 37:18.140
The fluid dynamics perform the math, and the physical friction of the fluid being forced through the microchannels generates ambient thermal energy,

37:18.460 --> 37:20.460
which radiates outward to heat the room.

37:20.640 --> 37:21.960
That is wild.

37:21.960 --> 37:22.660
It gets better.

37:23.120 --> 37:25.540
Example two is optical interference networks.

37:25.900 --> 37:26.700
What about light?

37:26.860 --> 37:27.680
Can light do math?

37:28.140 --> 37:35.880
Instead of sending electrical signals through silicon gates, you send beams of light through physical waveguides, beam splitters, and phase modulators.

37:36.080 --> 37:36.300
Okay.

37:36.580 --> 37:37.420
Lasers, basically.

37:37.900 --> 37:43.660
When light waves intersect, they interfere with each other, amplifying or canceling each other out based on their phase.

37:43.660 --> 37:51.600
It turns out that this physical interference perfectly mimics the complex matrix vector multiplication required to train neural networks.

37:51.720 --> 37:52.100
You're kidding.

37:52.300 --> 37:52.480
Nope.

37:53.080 --> 37:58.100
The overlapping light waves instantly perform the math at the speed of light with almost zero latency.

37:58.100 --> 38:04.660
The optical interface inevitably generates a small amount of thermal friction, which contributes to the thermal output of the node.

38:04.660 --> 38:05.180
Okay.

38:05.360 --> 38:06.500
Fluid and light make sense.

38:06.640 --> 38:07.400
I can picture that.

38:07.900 --> 38:09.340
But what about the heat itself?

38:09.660 --> 38:11.220
Can the heat act as a computer?

38:11.720 --> 38:11.940
Yes.

38:12.300 --> 38:14.420
Through phase change materials, or PCMs.

38:14.540 --> 38:15.540
Phase change materials.

38:16.040 --> 38:19.300
Think of a dense block of specialized wax inside the node.

38:19.780 --> 38:24.580
A PCM transitions between a solid and a liquid state at a very specific temperature.

38:24.580 --> 38:25.780
Okay, so it melts.

38:26.060 --> 38:26.220
Right.

38:26.440 --> 38:29.440
And when it melts, it absorbs a massive amount of latent heat.

38:29.760 --> 38:32.060
When it solidifies, it releases that heat.

38:32.180 --> 38:33.740
So it's basically a thermal battery.

38:34.100 --> 38:34.540
Exactly.

38:35.080 --> 38:36.340
But here's the crazy part.

38:36.860 --> 38:40.920
In a stage 4 node, this material isn't just a thermal battery for the home.

38:41.220 --> 38:44.340
The state of the material actually encodes data.

38:44.680 --> 38:44.980
What?

38:45.200 --> 38:48.000
A microscopic pocket of solid wax represents a zero.

38:48.460 --> 38:51.180
A pocket of liquid wax represents a one.

38:51.340 --> 38:52.100
So it's a hard drive.

38:52.220 --> 38:52.440
Yes.

38:52.440 --> 38:57.180
But instead of magnetic polarity on a spinning disk, the data is stored in the physical liquid

38:57.180 --> 38:58.680
or solid state of the material.

38:59.080 --> 38:59.440
Exactly.

38:59.900 --> 39:05.680
When the network needs to write data, it applies precise, microscopic heat to melt specific

39:05.680 --> 39:06.160
pockets.

39:07.140 --> 39:12.460
The act of writing data physically absorbs thermal energy, regulating the temperature of the node.

39:13.020 --> 39:15.480
The thermal state is the computational memory.

39:15.620 --> 39:17.120
That is genuinely mind-blowing.

39:17.380 --> 39:18.780
What about processing, though?

39:19.060 --> 39:20.520
Can heat actually calculate?

39:20.520 --> 39:22.600
Example 4 is thermal diffusion.

39:22.860 --> 39:23.100
Okay.

39:23.300 --> 39:25.480
Imagine a highly engineered metallic plate.

39:26.420 --> 39:31.320
If you apply a heat source to specific points along the edge of that plate, the thermal energy

39:31.320 --> 39:34.260
naturally diffuses across the metal, seeking equilibrium.

39:34.540 --> 39:34.820
Right.

39:35.040 --> 39:35.920
Heat spreads out.

39:36.060 --> 39:40.160
The way that heat spreads and settles into a steady state temperature gradient naturally

39:40.160 --> 39:44.020
solves a highly complex differential equation called a Laplace equation.

39:44.180 --> 39:45.060
Which is used for what?

39:45.400 --> 39:48.820
Physicists use it to map potential fields in electromagnetism and fluid flow.

39:48.820 --> 39:53.340
We normally use massive digital supercomputers to simulate how heat spreads.

39:54.100 --> 39:55.540
Stage 4 says skip the simulation.

39:55.640 --> 39:56.600
It's do the real thing.

39:56.680 --> 39:56.980
Exactly.

39:57.140 --> 40:01.480
Just apply heat to the physical plate, let the thermodynamics reach equilibrium, and use

40:01.480 --> 40:03.100
thermal cameras to read the temperature gradient.

40:03.680 --> 40:05.100
Reality does the math for you.

40:05.340 --> 40:08.240
The heat solves the math problem simply by acting like heat.

40:08.440 --> 40:11.740
And then that heat naturally bleeds off the plate to warm my house.

40:11.980 --> 40:12.460
Precisely.

40:12.720 --> 40:14.160
And the final example is acoustic.

40:14.160 --> 40:17.040
Resonant acoustic chambers.

40:17.320 --> 40:17.800
Sound.

40:18.000 --> 40:21.820
These are physical cavities engineered into the node, acting as sound insulation for the

40:21.820 --> 40:22.080
building.

40:22.620 --> 40:25.540
But the internal geometry of the cavity is shaped like a prism.

40:26.220 --> 40:30.560
Just as a glass prism physically splits white light into a rainbow of distinct colors,

40:31.160 --> 40:36.180
these acoustic chambers physically split complex audio signals into their distinct frequency

40:36.180 --> 40:36.620
bands.

40:36.880 --> 40:37.900
Spectral decomposition.

40:37.900 --> 40:38.380
Yes.

40:39.160 --> 40:43.440
You feed a complex noise into the chamber, and the physical shape of the room sorts the

40:43.440 --> 40:44.000
sound weights.

40:44.700 --> 40:48.580
The acoustic friction of the sound vibrating the walls dampens the noise for the homeowner

40:48.580 --> 40:51.560
while generating microscopic amounts of thermal energy.

40:52.060 --> 40:57.040
Okay, when you lay out the physical mechanisms, the water, the light, the wax, the metal plate,

40:57.140 --> 41:00.240
the sound, it suddenly makes perfect, terrifying sense.

41:00.460 --> 41:04.040
But how do we even measure the efficiency of something so alien?

41:04.040 --> 41:10.500
Fliction introduces a metric called functional density, denoted as F. The formula is simple.

41:11.020 --> 41:16.560
Functional density equals the sum of all useful services provided by an object divided by its

41:16.560 --> 41:17.340
physical mass.

41:17.460 --> 41:17.640
Okay.

41:18.440 --> 41:22.900
Think about a traditional server blade. It provides one service. Digital calculation.

41:23.580 --> 41:28.880
Its F-score is one. A traditional basement boiler provides one service. Thermal heat.

41:29.360 --> 41:30.540
Its F-score is one.

41:30.640 --> 41:32.340
Now stage three GPU node.

41:32.340 --> 41:35.700
It provides digital computation plus heat. Its F-score is two.

41:35.800 --> 41:36.580
That's stage four.

41:36.740 --> 41:40.220
A stage four node has an almost incomprehensible functional density.

41:40.980 --> 41:46.060
Within the exact same physical mass, it is providing fluid logic routing, optical matrix

41:46.060 --> 41:50.860
multiplication, thermal data storage, thermal diffusion processing, acoustic signal filtering,

41:51.320 --> 41:52.500
and baseline space heating.

41:52.600 --> 41:54.200
It's doing everything all at once.

41:54.220 --> 41:57.920
It is extracting the absolute maximum utility from the physical substrate.

41:57.920 --> 42:02.360
It completely annihilates the boundary between thermodynamics and information theory.

42:02.660 --> 42:06.780
It sounds like pure science fiction, honestly. Is there any precedent for this? Has humanity

42:06.780 --> 42:08.460
ever built something like this before?

42:08.840 --> 42:09.340
We have.

42:10.000 --> 42:13.740
And fliction provides crucial historical context to prove this isn't fantasy.

42:13.740 --> 42:20.560
Long before the silicon microchip monopolized our definition of a computer, we used physical

42:20.560 --> 42:21.560
co-computation.

42:21.560 --> 42:22.340
Like what?

42:22.700 --> 42:27.920
Think of the mechanical differential analyzers from the 1930s rooms, full of physical gears,

42:28.320 --> 42:29.280
shafts, and pulleys.

42:29.340 --> 42:29.880
Oh, right.

42:29.880 --> 42:34.380
The physical rotation and interlocking of the metal gears didn't represent the math.

42:34.700 --> 42:35.640
It was the math.

42:36.060 --> 42:38.280
They used them to calculate artillery trajectories.

42:38.420 --> 42:39.340
Analog computers.

42:39.500 --> 42:39.880
Exactly.

42:40.240 --> 42:46.400
But the most stunning example Fliction cites is the MONIAC, built by Bill Phillips in 1949.

42:46.720 --> 42:47.760
The MONIAC?

42:47.800 --> 42:48.600
What did that do?

42:48.720 --> 42:53.900
It was a massive, physical hydraulic computer designed to model the macroeconomic state of

42:53.900 --> 42:54.580
the United Kingdom.

42:54.860 --> 42:55.420
Hydraulic?

42:55.640 --> 42:56.320
Like water?

42:56.500 --> 42:56.780
Yes.

42:56.780 --> 43:00.720
It consisted of large, clear tanks and pipes filled with colored water.

43:01.340 --> 43:03.120
The water physically represented money.

43:03.280 --> 43:03.780
Oh, wow.

43:04.180 --> 43:05.940
A tank at the top represented the treasury.

43:06.680 --> 43:10.700
Pipes leading down represented government spending, taxation, and foreign investment.

43:11.160 --> 43:12.880
Physical valves controlled the flow rates.

43:12.960 --> 43:13.380
That's brutal.

43:13.400 --> 43:18.380
If you wanted to simulate the economic impact of lowering taxes, you physically opened a

43:18.380 --> 43:22.360
valve and watched how the water levels of the economy rose and fell in different tanks

43:22.360 --> 43:23.140
across the system.

43:23.240 --> 43:25.600
The flow of the water was the economic computation.

43:25.600 --> 43:26.980
It was tangible math.

43:27.180 --> 43:27.620
Exactly.

43:28.360 --> 43:31.020
What Fliction is proposing is not a speculative invention.

43:31.380 --> 43:32.160
It is a synthesis.

43:33.060 --> 43:38.780
It is taking the profound, forgotten legacy of physical analog computing, fusing it with

43:38.780 --> 43:44.540
21st century material science and embedding it directly into the structural walls and HVAC

43:44.540 --> 43:45.820
systems of our cities.

43:46.040 --> 43:51.600
As Fliction writes, the heater that thinks is not a poetic formulation, it is a precise physical

43:51.600 --> 43:52.100
description.

43:52.100 --> 43:52.780
Yes.

43:53.320 --> 43:56.520
In conventional silicon computing, heat is the ultimate enemy.

43:56.780 --> 43:57.660
It degrades hardware.

43:58.320 --> 44:02.420
In the xylomorphic node, heat is simply one of the physical dimensions through which the

44:02.420 --> 44:03.080
thinking occurs.

44:03.160 --> 44:04.700
Okay, I am fascinated by the physics.

44:05.200 --> 44:06.580
It's mathematically elegant.

44:06.920 --> 44:08.780
It maximizes functional density.

44:09.020 --> 44:12.100
It solves two civilizational energy crises simultaneously.

44:12.480 --> 44:13.060
It really does.

44:13.060 --> 44:16.100
So here is the multi-trillion dollar question.

44:17.240 --> 44:22.720
If the physics work and the economics work, why don't I have a fluid logic heating plate

44:22.720 --> 44:23.860
in my wall right now?

44:24.020 --> 44:25.200
What is stopping us?

44:25.440 --> 44:30.340
That brings us to segment six, which is unfortunately the most sobering section of the research.

44:30.460 --> 44:31.360
Of course it is.

44:31.580 --> 44:33.300
The roadblock is not the math.

44:33.880 --> 44:36.140
The roadblock is not the laws of thermodynamics.

44:36.640 --> 44:39.840
The roadblock is human bureaucracy and institutional friction.

44:39.840 --> 44:42.000
Ah, the Department of Missing Forms.

44:42.060 --> 44:42.500
Exactly.

44:43.220 --> 44:48.660
Think about the nightmare of trying to classify a xylomorphic node within our current legal

44:48.660 --> 44:49.780
and economic systems.

44:50.420 --> 44:54.560
It crosses four completely distinct, heavily fortified silos.

44:54.740 --> 44:55.500
Okay, lay them out.

44:55.620 --> 45:00.320
First, it generates heat, which means it falls under residential HVAC codes, municipal building

45:00.320 --> 45:02.260
permits, and fire safety regulations.

45:02.360 --> 45:02.600
Right.

45:02.600 --> 45:07.460
Second, it processes network data, which means it involves the commercial IT sector, cloud

45:07.460 --> 45:10.100
computing contracts, and data privacy laws.

45:10.200 --> 45:11.460
Which is a whole different world.

45:11.600 --> 45:16.500
Third, it draws fluctuating power from the wall, making it a distributed energy resource

45:16.500 --> 45:19.980
that must interface with the heavily regulated electrical grid.

45:20.160 --> 45:21.720
T-tility companies are going to love that.

45:21.780 --> 45:22.460
Oh, they'll hate it.

45:23.040 --> 45:28.320
And fourth, it sits in your living room, making it a consumer appliance subject to manufacturing

45:28.320 --> 45:29.660
standards and warranties.

45:29.660 --> 45:33.320
I'm picturing an amphibian trying to apply for a passport.

45:33.660 --> 45:34.700
That's a good way to look at it.

45:34.820 --> 45:35.000
Right.

45:35.580 --> 45:41.140
The government bureaucracy has a form for a fish and a form for a mammal, but it literally

45:41.140 --> 45:45.160
does not possess the administrative capacity to process a frog.

45:45.320 --> 45:46.360
That is exactly it.

45:46.560 --> 45:52.360
The inspector showing up to certify my furnace does not know how to evaluate the safety of

45:52.360 --> 45:54.960
a cryptographic phase change memory drive.

45:54.960 --> 46:01.660
And the IT technician managing cloud security protocols has absolutely no idea how to bleed

46:01.660 --> 46:05.160
the pressure out of a residential hydronic fluid logic loop.

46:05.560 --> 46:08.320
The manufacturing and maintenance logistics alone are staggering.

46:09.040 --> 46:11.260
Current high-performance processors are fragile.

46:11.520 --> 46:11.820
Very.

46:11.960 --> 46:17.400
They're designed to live their entire operational lives in sterile, hyper-controlled air-conditioned

46:17.400 --> 46:17.980
data centers.

46:18.220 --> 46:22.080
They require perfectly filtered air and specialized engineers on hand.

46:22.200 --> 46:23.740
But the real world is messy.

46:23.740 --> 46:25.080
It's extremely messy.

46:25.240 --> 46:27.440
A residential basement in New Brunswick is humid.

46:27.740 --> 46:28.420
It's dusty.

46:28.840 --> 46:29.460
There are spiders.

46:29.880 --> 46:34.400
If a stage 4 node breaks down in the middle of a blizzard, you cannot wait two weeks for

46:34.400 --> 46:36.580
a specialized silicon engineer to fly in.

46:36.640 --> 46:36.840
Right.

46:36.960 --> 46:42.400
A local HVAC technician needs to be able to drive a van to your house, take a wrench, and

46:42.400 --> 46:43.520
physically repair the unit.

46:44.100 --> 46:47.200
Flicken argues we need a totally different hardware trajectory.

46:47.200 --> 46:54.220
We need to move away from fragile, high-density power and toward rugged, modular, field-serviceable

46:54.220 --> 46:54.780
systems.

46:54.980 --> 46:58.780
It's like we've invented the warp drive, but we're trying to get it licensed by the Department

46:58.780 --> 46:59.660
of Horse Carriages.

46:59.980 --> 47:03.120
The old rule simply cannot accommodate the new geometry.

47:03.120 --> 47:09.680
Fliction beautifully conceptualizes this friction by distinguishing between a substitution narrative

47:09.680 --> 47:11.740
and a transformation narrative.

47:11.980 --> 47:13.120
Define the difference for me.

47:13.460 --> 47:17.920
Almost all of our current strategies for the green energy transition are substitution narratives.

47:18.460 --> 47:22.960
We take an existing piece of infrastructure, we keep its function exactly the same, and we

47:22.960 --> 47:24.740
just swap the underlying power source.

47:24.820 --> 47:28.100
Like swapping a coal-fired power plant for a field of solar panels.

47:28.100 --> 47:31.840
Or swapping a gas-guzzling sedan for an electric vehicle.

47:32.160 --> 47:34.440
Or swapping a gas furnace for a heat pump.

47:35.240 --> 47:38.960
The physical shape and social function of the system do not change.

47:39.200 --> 47:41.300
A car is still a car, isolated on a road.

47:41.880 --> 47:44.520
A heater is still a heater, isolated in a basement.

47:44.800 --> 47:46.000
It's a one-to-one replacement.

47:46.760 --> 47:51.680
Bureaucrats love substitution narratives because they fit perfectly into existing spreadsheets.

47:51.860 --> 47:54.880
You don't have to rewrite the zoning laws, you just change the fuel type.

47:55.020 --> 47:55.400
Exactly.

47:55.600 --> 47:57.760
But a transformation narrative is entirely different.

47:58.100 --> 48:00.400
It does not simply swap a power source.

48:00.800 --> 48:05.580
It fundamentally reorganizes the spatial and social layout of society.

48:05.740 --> 48:07.680
Give me an example of a transformation narrative.

48:07.920 --> 48:12.000
The classic historical example Flitchin uses is the electrification of the factory.

48:12.320 --> 48:13.060
Walk me through that.

48:13.240 --> 48:15.760
How did electricity change the shape of the factory?

48:16.400 --> 48:21.020
During the steam era, a factory was powered by a single, massive steam engine.

48:21.020 --> 48:31.280
Because you had to physically transfer the mechanical power from the engine to the loons and lathes, every machine had to be connected by physical leather belts and drive shafts to that central engine.

48:31.340 --> 48:32.340
So they had to be close together.

48:32.700 --> 48:33.300
Very close.

48:33.840 --> 48:41.280
This physical constraint meant factories had to be built tall, stacked vertically, to keep every floor close to the central power source.

48:41.280 --> 48:41.760
Okay.

48:41.760 --> 48:45.140
But when electric motors were introduced, everything changed.

48:45.580 --> 48:48.480
You didn't just swap the steam engine for an electric generator.

48:49.360 --> 48:53.520
Electricity could be transmitted effortlessly through wires over long distances.

48:53.720 --> 48:53.960
Right.

48:54.280 --> 48:56.000
Suddenly, machines could be placed anywhere.

48:56.000 --> 49:01.880
The factory layout flattened out into sprawling, horizontal, single-story assembly lines.

49:02.100 --> 49:02.720
Oh, wow.

49:03.040 --> 49:14.040
And because they were no longer bound to a central steam plant, factories moved out of crowded urban centers and into the sprawling suburbs, which fundamentally and permanently altered urban geography.

49:14.040 --> 49:21.300
The new technology dissolved the old physical constraint, and the entire shape of human society deformed to follow the new rules.

49:21.560 --> 49:21.780
Yes.

49:22.100 --> 49:25.040
And the xylomorphic proposal is a transformation narrative.

49:25.040 --> 49:29.980
We are not just trying to invent a slightly greener heater or a slightly more efficient server.

49:30.260 --> 49:38.060
We are trying to engineer an object that performs thermal regulation as a natural, integrated co-product of a global computational process.

49:38.220 --> 49:42.060
It renders the concept of the isolated data center obsolete.

49:42.200 --> 49:42.880
I really doubt.

49:42.920 --> 49:45.000
Which brings us full circle back to the forest.

49:45.020 --> 49:46.860
Oh, the arboreal analogy.

49:47.020 --> 49:49.360
The intelligence of a forest is not centralized.

49:50.180 --> 49:54.700
A tree does not have a brain in its roots and a separate heating department in its leaves.

49:54.700 --> 49:55.100
No.

49:55.100 --> 50:03.980
A single leaf is simultaneously a solar panel, a carbon gas exchanger, a hydraulic water regulator, and a structural element.

50:04.640 --> 50:12.440
The resilience and efficiency of the forest emerged from the decentralized coordination of millions of these multifunctional nodes.

50:12.440 --> 50:15.720
That is the existence proof Flickie leaves us with.

50:15.720 --> 50:16.160
Hmm.

50:16.160 --> 50:22.140
The paper is a rigorous mathematical demonstration that a completely different architecture is physically possible.

50:22.780 --> 50:30.680
The only question is whether we are willing to endure the agonizing institutional work of tearing down our bureaucratic silos to build it.

50:30.680 --> 50:33.260
Okay, let's pull back and synthesize this journey.

50:33.340 --> 50:33.800
Let's do it.

50:34.000 --> 50:42.740
We started by diagnosing the separated substrate problem, the bizarre, highly inefficient historical accident of isolating computation from heat generation.

50:42.740 --> 50:50.040
We explored how the millions of miles of fiber optic cables currently beneath our feet perfectly map onto human heat demand.

50:50.780 --> 51:00.180
We ran the numbers on how routing latency-tolerant bash workloads to distributed residential nodes could carve a massive dent out of our winter heating grids.

51:00.520 --> 51:01.800
We really went into the weeds there.

51:01.860 --> 51:02.240
We did.

51:02.240 --> 51:10.780
We took the dizzying leap into stage four physical co-computation, where the movement of light, fluid, and heat literally is the math.

51:11.100 --> 51:11.740
Mind-bending.

51:11.980 --> 51:21.460
And finally, we confronted the reality that the biggest obstacle to this post-carbon future isn't the laws of physics, but the rigidity of our own categorical thinking.

51:21.700 --> 51:24.220
It is a sweeping, profound vision.

51:24.220 --> 51:34.680
It challenges us to stop accepting the waste generated by single output systems and to start demanding infrastructure that respects the totality of the physical energy it consumes.

51:35.080 --> 51:43.020
It fundamentally changes how I look at every machine in my house, and it leaves me with one final, deeply provocative thought that I want to explore with you.

51:43.120 --> 51:43.680
Okay, I'm listening.

51:43.680 --> 52:03.960
If this transformation actually happens, if the future of infrastructure truly becomes xylomorphic, if the fluid loops in your walls, the phase-change materials in your ceiling, and the acoustic geometry of your living room are all physically computing the needs of the global network as a literal side effect of keeping you warm and sheltered from the winter.

52:04.120 --> 52:04.340
Yeah.

52:04.740 --> 52:10.360
At what point do we stop thinking of ourselves as simply living inside houses equipped with smart devices?

52:10.360 --> 52:15.040
At what point do we realize we're actually living inside the computer itself?

52:15.260 --> 52:15.780
Oh, wow.

52:15.960 --> 52:29.800
If the heater that thinks becomes the invisible baseline of human existence, what kind of relationship will you develop with a building that knows exactly how to comfort you precisely because it spends all winter quietly thinking about the rest of the world?

52:30.140 --> 52:32.580
It redefines the concept of shelter entirely.

52:32.940 --> 52:36.340
The building is no longer a passive barrier against nature.

52:36.840 --> 52:39.880
It is an active, cognitive participant in the ecosystem.

52:39.880 --> 52:41.180
It really is.

52:41.260 --> 52:43.780
Keep questioning the single output systems around you.

52:44.100 --> 52:46.480
Keep looking for those hidden, separated substrates.

52:46.620 --> 52:48.280
And thank you for bringing this paper to us.

52:48.320 --> 52:50.180
It's been an incredible deep dive.

